(1) Let $x\geq y\geq z$. Then $3x^3\geq2001$ and $x^3<2001$, so
$12\geq x\geq9$.
Also $x^3\equiv0,\pm1\pmod9$, but $2001\equiv3\pmod9$.
This forces $x\equiv y\equiv z\equiv1\pmod9$.
The only such number in the interval for $x$ is $10$, so $x=10$.
Then $y^3+z^3=1001$. Arguing similarly gives $10\geq y\geq8$;
only $y=10$ satisfies the congruence, and this puts $z=1$.
Thus the solutions are the permutations of $(10,10,1)$.

